WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.112
A workspace full of tables
is still a warehouse.

2
00:00:03.112 --> 00:00:07.825
Rules, incidents and automation are
what turn it into a detection engine.

3
00:00:07.825 --> 00:00:12.643
And the program's rule is strict: no source
counts as monitored until an enabled

4
00:00:12.643 --> 00:00:17.387
rule reads it, and no rule counts until
its output reaches a queue someone owns.

5
00:00:17.387 --> 00:00:20.787
The engine also sets a floor
on how fast you can detect.

6
00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:23.688
Scheduled rules run every
few minutes at best,

7
00:00:23.688 --> 00:00:27.047
near-real-time rules are
faster but limited in number,

8
00:00:27.047 --> 00:00:31.626
and behavioral baselines are slower
by design; the slide has the figures.

9
00:00:31.626 --> 00:00:37.563
Defender XDR detects what its own sensors
see on endpoints, identities and email.

10
00:00:37.563 --> 00:00:42.712
Sentinel rules cover what Defender
can't see, like DNS and other clouds.

11
00:00:42.712 --> 00:00:46.275
Then prepare for the assessor's
test: show a recent alert,

12
00:00:46.275 --> 00:00:50.225
the incident it opened, and what
was done, from alert to closure.

13
00:00:50.225 --> 00:00:52.250
Next, the federal clocks.
